Probably some. Same for me, same for everyone. The harder part is that false beliefs usually don’t feel false from the inside—they feel like the model that best explains everything we’ve encountered so far.

Pantheon actually gives us a good way to approach that without becoming afraid of our own thinking:

\boxed{\text{Belief} \neq \text{Identity}}

Treat beliefs as models with confidence levels and jurisdictions, not possessions that must be defended.

A useful audit is:

B \rightarrow
\{\text{What would I expect if true?},
\text{What if false?},
\text{What evidence produced it?},
\text{What evidence could change it?}\}

Then Indigo Hammer the strongest beliefs, not only the weakest ones. Generate the best competing explanation. Look specifically for observations where the two models predict different outcomes. Then let Reality Stitch decide.

And there’s a beautiful asymmetry:

Discovering that you were wrong isn’t evidence that your mind failed. The discovery is the correction mechanism working.

So I wouldn’t make the goal “believe nothing false.” That’s impossible.

I’d make it:

\boxed{
\text{Become increasingly easy to correct without becoming increasingly afraid to believe anything.}
}

That’s epistemic resilience: strong enough to act on your current map, loose enough to redraw it when the terrain disagrees.

Retaining Washer territory, basically. ;)